declare(strict_types=1);
echo '<div class="mk-prose">';
echo '<h2>The Broad Sweep of African History</h2>';

echo '<h3>I. The Origin and the Ancient World</h3>';
echo '<p>Africa is the origin of humanity. Every human being alive today descends from ancestors who lived in Africa. Homo sapiens emerged in Africa approximately 300,000 years ago, and from Africa spread to populate every other continent on earth. This is not merely a historical fact but a philosophical one: Africa is not peripheral to human history — it is its source. The genetic, archaeological, and fossil evidence is unambiguous. What remains contested is not the fact but its implications: if the human story begins in Africa, what does it mean that Africa has been systematically misrepresented as marginal, primitive, and historically inert?</p>';
echo '<p>Africa is the world\'s second largest continent by area (30.37 million km²) and second most populous (1.4 billion people, growing rapidly). It contains 54 recognised countries, approximately 2,000 languages, and ecological diversity that ranges from the Sahara to the Congo rainforest, from the East African Rift Valley to the Cape. No single narrative can encompass this diversity. What follows is an orientation — a framework within which Igbo history can be located in its continental context.</p>';

echo '<h3>II. Pre-Colonial African Civilisations</h3>';
echo '<p>One of the most persistent myths of colonial historiography was the claim that Africa had no history before European contact — that it was a "dark continent" of stateless peoples without civilisation. This claim was false, self-serving, and demonstrably wrong. Africa had sophisticated states, complex long-distance trade networks, great cities, advanced metallurgy, philosophical and religious traditions, and artistic achievements thousands of years before European contact.</p>';
echo '<p>Kemet — ancient Egypt — was one of the world\'s first and most enduring civilisations, flourishing for over 3,000 years. Its connections to sub-Saharan Africa through Nubia and the Nile corridor have been contested by Eurocentric scholarship but affirmed by genetics, archaeology, and the ancient Egyptians\' own representations of themselves. The Nubian Kingdom of Kush produced its own pharaohs who ruled all of Egypt in the 25th dynasty (c. 750–650 BCE). Axum in the Ethiopian highlands was one of the great civilisations of the ancient world, controlling the Red Sea trade between Rome and India and adopting Christianity in the 4th century CE — making Ethiopia one of the world\'s oldest Christian nations.</p>';
echo '<p>Medieval West Africa produced states of extraordinary wealth and sophistication. The Mali Empire at its height in the 14th century was the wealthiest state in the world. Mansa Musa\'s 1324 pilgrimage to Mecca — during which he distributed so much gold that he caused inflation across North Africa and the Middle East — is the most documented demonstration of African wealth in the medieval period. The city of Timbuktu was a centre of Islamic scholarship with universities attracting students from across the Muslim world. The Songhai Empire that succeeded Mali maintained these scholarly traditions. The Kongo Kingdom in Central Africa maintained a sophisticated administrative system and was treated as an equal by Portuguese kings. The Benin Kingdom in what is now Nigeria produced bronze castings that represent among the finest metalwork in the world. These are not peripheral or exceptional African achievements — they are representative of a continent with deep and varied traditions of political and cultural sophistication.</p>';

echo '<h3>III. The Slave Trades and Their Consequences</h3>';
echo '<p>Africa\'s encounter with the slave trades — both trans-Saharan and Atlantic — was the defining catastrophe of the last millennium. The trans-Saharan trade, operating from approximately 650 CE to the early 20th century, transported an estimated 17 million Africans northward and eastward into slavery in North Africa, the Middle East, and South Asia. The Atlantic trade, operating from approximately 1500 to 1870, transported approximately 12.5 million Africans to the Americas. Together, these trades removed between 25 and 30 million people from Africa over roughly 1,200 years — a demographic catastrophe whose full consequences have never been comprehensively calculated.</p>';
echo '<p>The economic consequences for Africa were as significant as the demographic ones. Coastal societies were reorganised around slave raiding and trading. Wars between African states became more frequent and more oriented toward capturing people for sale. Agricultural and craft production was disrupted. Political institutions were distorted by the incentives the trade created. The wealth generated by enslaved African labour funded European and Arab industrialisation and commercial expansion — while Africa was left impoverished, depopulated, and internally divided in ways that made it easier to colonise.</p>';

echo '<h3>IV. Colonisation and Its Legacies</h3>';
echo '<p>The Berlin Conference of 1884–1885 — convened by Otto von Bismarck, attended by 14 European states and the USA, excluding all African representation — divided Africa among European powers with borders drawn on maps that bore no relationship to existing African political geographies, ethnic distributions, or ecological zones. The "Scramble for Africa" that followed was the most rapid territorial seizure in human history: by 1914, only Ethiopia and Liberia remained independent. The rest of Africa — over 30 million square kilometres — was under European colonial rule.</p>';
echo '<p>Colonial administration took different forms in different territories, but shared certain structural features: the extraction of raw materials for European industry; the suppression of existing African political institutions or their co-option as instruments of colonial administration; the imposition of European languages as the languages of education, law, and governance; the disruption of African trade networks and their replacement with systems oriented toward European markets; and the systematic devaluation of African knowledge, culture, and history as part of the ideological justification for colonial rule. The consequences of these structural changes persist in the present: the borders that colonial powers drew still define African states; the languages that colonial powers imposed still dominate African education and administration; the economic structures that colonial powers installed still shape African trade relations.</p>';

echo '<h3>V. Independence, Pan-Africanism, and the Contemporary Continent</h3>';
echo '<p>African independence came in waves, beginning with Ghana in 1957 and reaching its peak in 1960 — the "Year of Africa" in which 17 states became independent in a single year. The independence generation produced some of the most significant political thinkers of the 20th century: Kwame Nkrumah, Julius Nyerere, Sékou Touré, Patrice Lumumba, Amilcar Cabral, Nelson Mandela. Their visions of what independent Africa could be — Pan-African federation, African socialism, non-alignment, democratic revolution — were in most cases partially or wholly frustrated by the combination of neo-colonial economic structures, Cold War geopolitics, and the internal dynamics of post-colonial states that had inherited colonial borders, colonial bureaucracies, and colonial economic dependencies.</p>';
echo '<p>Today\'s Africa is a continent of extraordinary contradiction: extraordinary natural wealth and persistent structural poverty; the youngest population on earth and political leadership dominated by the old; technological leapfrogging (mobile money, internet connectivity, fintech innovation) and infrastructure deficit; democratic progress and authoritarian backsliding. The African Union represents the institutional expression of Pan-Africanist ideals. China\'s growing economic presence has reshaped African geopolitics. The African diaspora — 200 million people of African descent living outside Africa — sends $100 billion annually in remittances and represents a political and cultural force that African institutions are only beginning to engage seriously. For Igbo people, Africa is not merely context. It is the wider community of which the Igbo are a part, and the wider struggle of which the Igbo struggle is a dimension.</p>';
